WEBVTT

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:12.710
This is Hacker Public Radio Episode 4740 for Friday 2 October 2026.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.710
Today's show is entitled, Robert A. Heinlein in the 1960s.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.710
It is part of the series Science Fiction and Fantasy.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.710
It is hosted by Ahuka and is about 12 minutes long.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:30.710
It carries a clean flag. The summary is, in the 1960s Heinlein reached his peak.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:43.700
Hello, this is Hoka. Welcome to You to Hacker Public Radio in another exciting episode.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.020
exciting episode in our science fiction and fantasy series, continuing our look at

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:56.660
Heinlein and now taking a look at what he did in the 1960s. Now Heinlein had

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.700
interests that he tended to keep hidden in the early years while he was

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.980
establishing himself as a writer, particularly as he was feeding the lucrative

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:13.220
juvenile market. These included things like sex, nudism, and unusual

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.780
political and religious views.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:22.260
But when Scripner's rejected Starship Troopers, he not only abandoned writing juveniles, but

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.740
decided he could be a lot more open about his views.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.580
And this resulted in his most famous novel.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.820
This is the one novel that people who are not science fiction fans are likely to have read,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.460
particularly in the 1960s.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:43.620
And I am referring, of course, to Stranger in a Strange Land, 1961.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.300
Now this novel won just about every award possible.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:54.220
Hugo, Locust, Prometheus, it was the first science fiction novel to crack the New York

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.020
Times bestseller list, and the Library of Congress named it one of the 88 books that

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.120
shaped America.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.940
and it even introduced a new word to the English language, grok.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:13.780
The stranger in the title is Valentine Michael Smith, an infant born to one of the astronauts,

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.340
and then raised by Martians.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.140
He is brought back to Earth by a later mission, and his adventures in this strange land are

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.220
what the book is mostly about.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.340
But in so doing, Heinlein writes a satire on religion, and also, espouses a very different

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.860
approach to love and sexuality, then was considered proper at the time.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.820
And this attracted the hippies of the 1960s, for whom it became a Bible,

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:46.220
much to the annoyance of Heinlein, who did not appreciate them knocking on his door.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.220
Now the book had the original title of the heretic,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.860
and was intended to challenge social norms about religion and monogamy.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.300
Heinlein's own take on the reaction to the novel was that he was surprised

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.100
that some readers thought the book described how he believed society should be organized,

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.100
explaining,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:13.100
I was not giving answers. I was trying to shake the reader loose from preconceptions

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.740
and induce him to think for himself along new and fresh lines.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.260
In consequence, each reader gets something different out of the book because he

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.180
himself supplies the answers.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.220
It's an invitation to think, not to believe.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:35.500
This book also exemplifies the characters that Heinlein managed to put into most of his stories.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:45.500
Michael is the naive young man who needs to learn better, and Jubal Harshaw is the wise old man who becomes his mentor.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:52.930
Then, Podkain of Mars, 1963.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:59.650
The hero went to this book, Podkain is a teenage girl, who was invited along with her

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:05.650
younger brother on a luxury cruise with their uncle Tom from their home on Mars to go to the

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.570
earth.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:14.970
We learn along the way that Mars was once a prison colony, kind of like Australia, and

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.970
that Podkain's family are of Maori ancestry.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.930
In the United States at least, the sweet count is colored and reflects Heinlein's subversion

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.650
of racist ideas in his works.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:34.210
Much of the travelogue on the luxury liner reflects Heinlein's own experiences as depicted

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:40.410
in his nonfiction book, Tramp Royale, which covers his own world's cruises.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:46.090
But old Uncle Tom is actually a diplomat using the kids as cover.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:51.410
And they get kidnapped by people who want him to take a certain position that talks to

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.890
be held at Luna.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:59.370
Podkane's brother Clark is portrayed as a genius but lacking certain qualities of empathy.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.240
and in the end he manages to get her killed.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:09.290
The publisher rejected this ending and insisted on one where she lived.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.530
Later Heinlein published a version with both endings and invited people to vote on which

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:18.610
one they preferred, and the one where she died won.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:24.210
Heinlein's own objection was that it was like changing the ending to Romeo and Juliet into

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:28.010
one in which they both lived happily ever after.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:34.200
Since then, the book has been published with Heinlein's preferred ending.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.640
Warfins of the Sky, 1963.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:45.740
This is basically a couple of novellas from 1941 put together as a complete novel.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:51.960
The first novella, universe, was recognized by the science fiction writers of America by

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.960
including it in their volume, the science fiction hall of fame volume 2.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:03.160
This is a story about a generation ship, i.e. a ship that will take generations to arrive

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.560
due to the speed of light limit.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.960
In this case, the people who understood what this was all about are long dead, and we learned

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:17.560
that at one point there was a mutiny and that the ship's officers were killed.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.960
In universe, the descendants of the remaining people think the ship is all there is as it

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.080
is the universe to them.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.120
In the second novella, common sense, a few people find some of the old records and work

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.040
out what the situation is.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:39.600
Then seeing a star growing larger ahead of them, they take a lifeboat and land on a habitable

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.800
moon of a giant planet.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.800
This is part of the future history, as Riesling and his Green Hills of Earth are brought up,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.000
and it is also referred to in Time Enough for Love when it is revealed that this ship

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.800
is the sister ship to the one the Howard family stole in Methusel's children.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:05.600
But being that this is from 1941, this is not show the polish and mastery of Heinlein's

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:10.980
other works of the 1960s.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:16.820
And then this Glory Road published in 1963.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:20.980
Some stories make you wonder if they're fantasy or if they are science fiction, and this is

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:21.980
one of them.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:27.660
Of course, Heinlein would just call it speculative fiction and not worry about it.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:32.580
A former soldier has just been discharged from a war in Southeast Asia.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:39.020
The country is not mentioned, but this is 1963, and Vietnam was just bubbling up in the people's

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.900
consciousness.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.940
With nothing to do, he meets up with a gorgeous woman who invites him on a quest.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:51.340
This takes them to other worlds and involves a certain amount of sword play, culminating in

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:57.900
sword fight against Cyrano de Bergerac. The woman turns out to be the Empress of many

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:04.820
worlds and the quest was to retrieve the egg of the phoenix, a cybernetic device containing

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:10.660
the memories and personalities of most of her predecessors, and now she has to absorb

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:17.780
all of this, which is fine for her but leaves our hero pretty useless. Finally he goes looking

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.980
for another quest.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:26.740
This is a really good novel, who was nominated for a Hugo, but it lost out to Clifford Simack's

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.740
way station.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.240
We'll probably get to discussing Simack at some point.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:37.460
He was an important writer with a distinct style.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:41.820
Then Farnham's Freehold, 1964.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:48.540
This novel attempts to be a satire on race relations, in my view it is a failure.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:55.860
A nuclear war happens, and a group of people in a fallout shelter find they have been transported

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:00.260
into the future, or is it an alternative universe?

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:05.500
In any event, the war pretty much destroyed the societies of the northern hemisphere,

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:10.580
leaving things open for the Africans and other Southerners to become dominant.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:15.780
And of course, the blacks enslaved the rights and reenact the worst excesses of Antebellum

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.940
American south. So let's just say when I'm in the mood to go back and reread

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:29.660
some Highline, this is not one I pick. But then there's the Moon is a harsh

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:39.160
mistress 1966. This is one of Highline's best novels set on a lunar colony. The

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:44.400
moon has been used as a penal colony, but of course most of the people there now

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.640
now are descendants of the people originally exiled there.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:54.240
The warden runs things, but as long as the grain shipments are on schedule, he doesn't

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.880
bother much with the affairs of the loonies.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.640
The background here is fascinating.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Describing a nearly anarchic society.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Men outnumber women two to one, leading to some interesting marriage customs,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
including something called line marriage, which is a type of group marriage.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:22.000
The wise old man role is played by Professor Bernardo de la Paz, also known as prof,

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:29.000
while the young man he mentors is Manuel Okele Davis, otherwise known as Manny.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:34.200
Manny is a computer technician who discovers the big mainframe computer that runs everything

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:43.800
has become sentient. And since the computer has the acronym H-O-L-M-E-S, or Holmes, he names it

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:51.800
Mycroft Holmes, Mike for short, who is Sherlock's smarter brother. They all become involved in a

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:57.560
revolution against the Earth government, which in theory the revolution is led by the

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:03.560
but in fact it's led by Mike. Since he's a computer and keeps his sentience a secret,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:08.560
he creates a persona called Adam Celine to be the figurehead.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:15.940
I said that the background of this lunar colony is fascinating in itself,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:18.940
but the book is also a discourse on the nature of government,

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:21.940
libertarianism, and what it means to be free.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:26.940
That was something Heinlein was prone to do, and it did not always work well.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:32.780
work well. In some of his last works, he tended to pontificate in a way that left the story behind.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:40.730
But in this novel, the story is interesting all the way through. This is the novel that popularized

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:47.050
the acronym TANSTAFL, which is short for, there ain't no such thing as a free lunch,

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:54.330
and it is the motto of the loonies. Hazel Stone from the Rolling Stones appears here as a 12-year-old

00:11:54.330 --> 00:12:00.250
girl involved in a revolution, and both she and Mike also appear in later works of Highline.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:07.450
I can recommend this book highly. This novel won a Hugo award, that's the one by the fans,

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:13.130
was nominated for a Nebula award, that's the science fiction writers,

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:21.930
was named one of the top 10 novels in the Locust Pole and won the Prometheus Hall of Fame award.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:30.570
